 ; Program for DME-DME navigation (aviation)
 ;
 ; DISCLAIMER:
 ; This program is designed for experimental navigation calculations over distances and at altitudes typical of general aviation (GA).
 ; It uses an approximate planar model of the Earth's surface, with corrections for the convergence of meridians towards the poles,
 ; rather than full spherical or ellipsoidal geometry.
 ; This approximation introduces systematic position errors that can become substantial over long distances.
 ; The magnitude of these errors depends on the geographical configuration and generally increases with distance.
 ; Consequently, the program is not suitable for the extended navigation distances typical of high-altitude airline operations.
 ; For educational and flight-simulation use only. Not suitable as a primary or safety-critical navigation aid in real aircraft.
 ;
 ; Operating conditions:
 ; Initialization and angle mode: Before first use, press D' to initialize the program. This also enforces Deg mode;
 ; do not use Rad or Grad. Press E' to calculate, not to initialize (see the BEWARE warning below).
 ; Station coordinates: Enter the geographical latitude and longitude of both DME stations in DDDMM.mmmmm format.
 ; Southern latitudes and western longitudes must be negative. The stations must have distinct positions;
 ; coincident stations do not provide a two-station position fix.
 ; Distances: Enter the two measured DME slant ranges in nautical miles (nm). The readings should refer to essentially the same aircraft position;
 ; aircraft movement between measurements is not corrected for. Negative distance inputs are treated as their absolute values.
 ; Optional height correction: Enter the aircraft's vertical height relative to each DME station's transmitting/receiving antenna, in feet,
 ; using SBR ( or SBR ), or SBR EE when both height differences are the same.
 ; These are not necessarily the aircraft's height above the terrain beneath it (AGL);
 ; if using altitudes above mean sea level (AMSL), subtract the corresponding station antenna elevation.
 ; The sign of a height difference is immaterial because the correction squares it.
 ; A height difference of zero leaves the measured slant range uncorrected.
 ; Planar approximation: The program converts corrected distances to equivalent latitude degrees and scales longitude differences
 ; by the cosine of the stations' mean latitude. This is an approximation intended for relatively short distances;
 ; its accuracy deteriorates over large geographical extents, near the poles, and for station pairs crossing the +/-180ø longitude discontinuity
 ; (related to the International Date Line, or IDL).
 ; Two possible positions: When the two distance circles intersect at two points, the program calculates both positions. LEFT and RIGHT are defined
 ; relative to the directed line from DME 1 to DME 2; they do not identify which position is the actual aircraft position.
 ; Additional position information/knowledge from the user is required to distinguish between the two. By choosing either LEFT or RIGHT,
 ; the user is giving this information.
 ; Rejection criteria:
 ; (a) If the absolute height difference (in feet, converted to nautical miles) is greater than the measured slant range (in nautical miles),
 ; which is physically impossible in reality (or a severe measurement error), the program rejects.
 ; The program then corrects each measured slant range for its associated height difference, obtaining a horizontal distance.
 ; If the horizontal distance is zero (indicating that the aircraft is directly above that station), there are no two distinct intersection points,
 ; which however are required for a valid fix.
 ; (b) When L is the horizontal distance between the two DME stations and d1 and d2 their respective corrected horizontal distances to the aircraft,
 ; the two distance circles can have five geometrical relationships:
 ; Intersection angle quality: Two intersections can still give an unreliable fix if the circles cross at a very shallow angle, understood as
 ; the angle between the two lines from the aircraft to each of the two DME stations.
 ; This angle is folded into the range 0ø-90ø, because sin(à) = sin(180ø-à); an obtuse intersection angle is equivalent to its supplementary acute angle.
 ; The program rejects a fix if the folded angle is less than 5ø. This rejects intersections near either external or internal tangency,
 ; even when the circles cross at two distinct points.
 ; The error margin amplification factor is calculated as the reciprocal of the sine of this folded angle.
 ;
 ; Interface:
 ;
 ; BEWARE:
 ; Contrary to my other programs, in this program the actual calculations are NOT carried out by pressing E, but by pressing E'!
 ; Subtle difference, huge consequence!
 ; If you get a non-blinking zero in both X and T, you have probably pressed E instead of E'!
 ; Press E' to calculate the positions. Then press D for the LEFT position or E for the RIGHT position.
 ; Also BEWARE that in order to initialize the program, you need to press D', and NOT E', or you may get meaningless or confusing results.
 ;
 ; A : input/output DME 1 latitude                       (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; B : input/output DME 1 longitude                      (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; C : input        DME 1 distance                       (nautical miles)
 ; D : output position LEFT  of DME1->DME2 directed line
 ; E : output position RIGHT of DME1->DME2 directed line
 ;
 ; A': input/output DME 2 latitude                       (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; B': input/output DME 2 longitude                      (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; C': input        DME 2 distance                       (nautical miles)
 ; D': initialize
 ; E': calculate positions
 ;
 ; C : also switches to "output invalid" mode
 ; C': also switches to "output invalid" mode
 ; D': also switches to "output invalid" mode
 ; E': switches to "OUTPUT IS VALID" mode -- if at least one position has been found
 ;
 ; D': erases registers 01-28, switches A B A' B' to input (C and C' are always input) and sets DEG mode
 ; E': calculates the possible positions of the aircraft, and switches A B A' B' to output (C and C' are always input)
 ;
 ; Output E':
 ; x (current display) = the acute intersection angle (0-90), or a blinking "9.9999999 99" if the fix is rejected
 ; t (test register)   = measure of the error margin amplification factor, or zero if the fix is rejected
 ;
 ; Output D:
 ; x (current display) = result position latitude  (LEFT of directed line DME1->DME2)  (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; t (test register)   = result position longitude (LEFT of directed line DME1->DME2)  (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; Zero is returned in both registers if no solution has been found by E' or output has been set to "invalid".
 ;
 ; Output E:
 ; x (current display) = result position latitude  (RIGHT of directed line DME1->DME2) (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; t (test register)   = result position longitude (RIGHT of directed line DME1->DME2) (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; Zero is returned in both registers if no solution has been found by E' or output has been set to "invalid".

 ; SBR ( : store aircraft's height above DME 1 (in feet) in register 17 for calculation of corrected slant range for DME 1 (mnemonic: ( comes first)
 ; SBR ) : store aircraft's height above DME 2 (in feet) in register 18 for calculation of corrected slant range for DME 2 (mnemonic: ( comes first)
 ; SBR EE: store aircraft's height above both DME 1 and 2 (if both are the same) in registers 17 and 18 for correction     (mnemonic: EE is "double")

 ; SBR = : display the intersection angle in degrees (0-90) and the error margin amplification factor in test register t
 ; Call SBR = only AFTER calling E' to display the intersection angle and the error margin amplification factor again,
 ; also in the case of a rejected calculation, if available (if not, zero and plus infinity will be shown as defaults).
 ; Mnemonic: check whether the calculation is ill-conditioned (e.g. for tangent circles, which are akin to parallel lines).

 ; Conversion functions DDDMM.mmmmm <=> DDD.ddddddd:
 ; SBR  .  = DDDMM.mmmmm => DDD.ddddddd (to decimal format)
 ; SBR +/- = DDD.ddddddd => DDDMM.mmmmm (to cockpit format)
 ; Output: current display and register 00

 ; Registers:
 ; 00 Contains the conversion result DDDMM.mmmmm <=> DDD.ddddddd from SBR . and SBR +/-
 ; 01 DME 1 latitude              (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; 02 DME 1 longitude             (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; 03 DME 1 distance              (nautical miles)
 ; 04 DME 2 latitude              (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; 05 DME 2 longitude             (DDDMM.mmmmm)
 ; 06 DME 2 distance              (nautical miles)
 ; 07 Result position L latitude  (DDD.ddddddd)
 ; 08 Result position L longitude (DDD.ddddddd)
 ; 09 Result position R latitude  (DDD.ddddddd)
 ; 10 Result position R longitude (DDD.ddddddd)
 ; 11 DME 1 latitude              (DDD.ddddddd) Register 01 + 10
 ; 12 DME 1 longitude             (DDD.ddddddd) Register 02 + 10
 ; 13 DME 1 distance / 60 = r1    (DDD.ddddddd) Register 03 + 10
 ; 14 DME 2 latitude              (DDD.ddddddd) Register 04 + 10
 ; 15 DME 2 longitude             (DDD.ddddddd) Register 05 + 10
 ; 16 DME 2 distance / 60 = r2    (DDD.ddddddd) Register 06 + 10
 ; 17 Height above DME 1          (feet) (optional, can improve accuracy)
 ; 18 Height above DME 2          (feet) (optional, can improve accuracy)
 ; 19 YY = (Y2 - Y1)                    later normalized / L
 ; 20 XX = (X2 - X1) * COS( (Y1+Y2)/2 ) later normalized / L
 ; 21 COS( (Y1+Y2)/2 )
 ; 22 Baseline length L (= distance between DME 1 and DME 2)
 ; 23 Baseline offset a = ( ( r1^2 - r2^2 ) / L + L ) / 2
 ; 24 First q = r1^2 - a^2, then h = sqrt( q )
 ; 25 First sin(theta), then acute intersection angle theta (0-90)
 ; 26 Error margin amplification factor
 ; 27 Intermediate h * YY
 ; 28 Intermediate h * XX
 ; 29 Scratchpad for conversions

